    {
        "age": null,
        "album": "",
        "author": "Sarah Law",
        "bookmarked": false,
        "comments": [],
        "date_created": "2026-11-19T16:33:59.530169+00:00",
        "date_dead_since": null,
        "date_published": "2026-11-19T10:30:04+00:00",
        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/f0ad732f-9949-408c-871d-a645d90d5068,1795102135148/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C223%2C4240%2C2385%29%3B' alt='Lake Superior Krampus' width='620' height='349' title=''/><p>While he was banned from a Santa Claus parade in southern Ontario, Krampus is expected to make appearances at several festive events in Thunder Bay this holiday season. Unlike jolly old Saint Nicholas who leaves presents for children at Christmastime, the half-goat, half-demon is known in European folklore for punishing misbehaving youth. Here's what drives Lake Superior Krampus, and why people in Thunder Bay can't get enough of him.</p>",
